const PEOPLE=[{"y":"The first Christmas","name":"Luke","life":"1st century","role":"Doctor and Gospel writer","place":"Bethlehem","xy":[834.5,213.5],"match":"The Gospel of Luke","bio":"Luke was a doctor and a travelling companion of the apostle Paul. He wrote the Gospel of Luke and the book of Acts after carefully checking the facts with eyewitnesses. Almost everything we know about the shepherds, the angels and the manger comes from his account."},{"y":"c. 180","name":"Irenaeus","life":"c. 130 – c. 202","role":"Bishop of Lyon","place":"Lyon, Gaul (France)","xy":[652.1,111.9],"match":"Irenaeus","bio":"Irenaeus grew up in Smyrna, where he heard Polycarp, who had known the apostle John. As bishop of Lyon he wrote Against Heresies to answer teachers who said the body was bad and God could never truly become human. He insisted Jesus really took our flesh to bring us home to God."},{"y":"c. 320","name":"Athanasius","life":"c. 296 – 373","role":"Bishop of Alexandria","place":"Alexandria, Egypt","xy":[803.3,217.1],"match":"Athanasius","bio":"Athanasius stood up for the truth that Jesus is fully God when much of the empire believed otherwise. He was sent into exile five times and kept going, which earned the saying \"Athanasius against the world\". His short book On the Incarnation is still one of the best ever written on why God became man."},{"y":"c. 350","name":"Cyril of Jerusalem","life":"c. 313 – 386","role":"Bishop of Jerusalem","place":"Jerusalem","xy":[834.6,212.9],"match":"Cyril of Jerusalem","bio":"Cyril taught new believers who were preparing to be baptised, in the very city where Jesus died and rose. His Catechetical Lectures, given in the Church of the Holy Sepulchre, walk step by step through the faith. They give us a window into how the early church taught its new members."},{"y":"c. 360","name":"Ephrem the Syrian","life":"c. 306 – 373","role":"Deacon and hymn writer","place":"Nisibis, Syria (Turkey)","xy":[865.6,174.4],"match":"Ephrem","bio":"Ephrem was a deacon who taught the faith through songs, writing hundreds of hymns for his church to sing, including choirs of women. He was called \"the Harp of the Spirit\". His Christmas hymns are full of wonder that the Mighty One became a little child."},{"y":"c. 380","name":"Gregory of Nazianzus","life":"c. 329 – 390","role":"Archbishop of Constantinople","place":"Constantinople (Istanbul)","xy":[791.3,145.9],"match":"Gregory of Nazianzus","bio":"Gregory was a quiet scholar pulled into leading the church in the empire's capital at a troubled time. His sermons on the Trinity were so clear that the church gave him the title \"the Theologian\". His Christmas sermon is one of the earliest we have from the Eastern church."},{"y":"c. 400","name":"Augustine","life":"354 – 430","role":"Bishop of Hippo","place":"Hippo, North Africa (Algeria)","xy":[670.3,175.8],"match":"Augustine","bio":"Augustine spent a restless youth chasing pleasure and ideas before he came to faith in Milan at 31. As a bishop in North Africa he wrote Confessions, the story of his life told as a prayer, and The City of God. No teacher after the apostles has shaped Western Christianity more."},{"y":"c. 450","name":"Leo the Great","life":"c. 400 – 461","role":"Bishop of Rome","place":"Rome","xy":[696.5,139.5],"match":"Leo","bio":"Leo led the church in Rome as the Western empire was falling apart. His letter, the Tome of Leo, helped the Council of Chalcedon in 451 say clearly that Jesus is one person, truly God and truly man. In 452 he went out to meet Attila the Hun, who turned his army back from Rome."},{"y":"c. 700s","name":"The O Antiphons","life":"By the 700s","role":"Ancient Advent prayers","place":"Monasteries across Europe","xy":[652.9,103.1],"match":"The O Antiphons","bio":"These seven short prayers were sung in monasteries each evening from December 17 to 23. Each calls on Jesus by a name from Isaiah: Wisdom, Lord, Root of Jesse, Key of David, Dayspring, King of Nations, Emmanuel. They became the hymn O Come, O Come, Emmanuel."},{"y":"1098","name":"Anselm","life":"1033 – 1109","role":"Archbishop of Canterbury","place":"Canterbury, England","xy":[630.9,73],"match":"Anselm","bio":"Anselm was born in the Italian Alps, became a monk in Normandy, and later Archbishop of Canterbury. He described his work as \"faith seeking understanding\". His book Why God Became Man asks why only someone who is both God and man could save us."},{"y":"c. 1140","name":"Bernard of Clairvaux","life":"1090 – 1153","role":"Abbot and preacher","place":"Clairvaux, France","xy":[651.5,94.9],"match":"Bernard","bio":"Bernard founded the monastery of Clairvaux and became the most famous preacher of his age. He wrote warmly about the love of Jesus; the hymn Jesus, the Very Thought of Thee comes from his tradition. Centuries later, both Luther and Calvin quoted him with respect."},{"y":"1521","name":"Martin Luther","life":"1483 – 1546","role":"Reformer","place":"The Wartburg, Germany","xy":[681.1,75.2],"match":"Martin Luther","bio":"Luther was a monk and Bible teacher whose 95 Theses in 1517 started the Reformation. After refusing to take back his writings in 1521 he was hidden in the Wartburg castle. There he translated the New Testament into German and wrote his Advent and Christmas sermons."},{"y":"1549","name":"Thomas Cranmer","life":"1489 – 1556","role":"Archbishop of Canterbury","place":"Canterbury, England","xy":[630.9,73],"match":"Thomas Cranmer","bio":"Cranmer was Archbishop of Canterbury under Henry VIII and Edward VI. He wrote the Book of Common Prayer, so ordinary English people could pray in their own language; its prayers are still used around the world. He was burned at the stake in Oxford in 1556 for his faith."},{"y":"1555","name":"John Calvin","life":"1509 – 1564","role":"Pastor and reformer","place":"Geneva, Switzerland","xy":[659.4,108.8],"match":"John Calvin","bio":"Calvin was a French lawyer who fled persecution and became the pastor of Geneva. His Institutes of the Christian Religion and his commentaries on almost the whole Bible shaped Protestant churches everywhere. Geneva trained pastors who carried the gospel across Europe."},{"y":"1563","name":"The Heidelberg Catechism","life":"1563","role":"A teaching guide in questions","place":"Heidelberg, Germany","xy":[672.8,86.1],"match":"The Heidelberg Catechism","bio":"Written by two young theologians, Zacharias Ursinus and Caspar Olevianus, for the churches of the Palatinate. Its 129 questions and answers begin with: What is your only comfort in life and in death? It is still one of the most loved summaries of the faith."},{"y":"1618","name":"Lancelot Andrewes","life":"1555 – 1626","role":"Bishop and Bible translator","place":"London","xy":[624.3,71.4],"match":"Lancelot Andrewes","bio":"Andrewes was one of the translators of the King James Bible and preached each Christmas before King James I. He was famous for slowly turning over single words of a verse until they shone. T. S. Eliot borrowed a line from one of his sermons for the poem Journey of the Magi."},{"y":"1692","name":"Thomas Watson","life":"c. 1620 – 1686","role":"Puritan pastor","place":"London","xy":[624.3,71.4],"match":"Thomas Watson","bio":"Watson was a London pastor who lost his church in 1662 for refusing to conform, and went on preaching in private homes. His sermons on the Shorter Catechism were published after his death as A Body of Divinity. He is loved for plain, vivid sentences that are easy to remember."},{"y":"1730s","name":"Jonathan Edwards","life":"1703 – 1758","role":"Pastor and theologian","place":"Northampton, Massachusetts","xy":[210.6,136.4],"match":"Jonathan Edwards","bio":"Edwards was a pastor in colonial Massachusetts and a leader of the Great Awakening revival. He is often called America's greatest theologian. He wrote with wonder about the beauty of Christ, and died weeks after becoming president of the college that became Princeton."},{"y":"c. 1740","name":"George Whitefield","life":"1714 – 1770","role":"Evangelist","place":"Gloucester, England","xy":[612.9,69],"match":"George Whitefield","bio":"Whitefield was born in an inn in Gloucester and became the best-known preacher of the 1700s. He preached in the open air to crowds of thousands in Britain and America, crossing the Atlantic again and again. Benjamin Franklin was his friend and printed his sermons."},{"y":"1830s","name":"John Henry Newman","life":"1801 – 1890","role":"Oxford preacher","place":"Oxford, England","xy":[618.2,69.7],"match":"John Henry Newman","bio":"Newman was the vicar of the university church in Oxford, where students packed in to hear his sermons. He later became a Catholic and a cardinal. His sermons, like the one on watching for Christ, are still read for their searching honesty."},{"y":"1855","name":"Charles Spurgeon","life":"1834 – 1892","role":"Pastor in London","place":"London","xy":[624.3,71.4],"match":"Charles Spurgeon","bio":"Spurgeon began preaching in London at 19 and was soon filling the largest halls in the city. He became known as the Prince of Preachers, and his sermons were printed every week and read around the world. He also founded an orphanage and a college for pastors."},{"y":"1859","name":"J. C. Ryle","life":"1816 – 1900","role":"Pastor, later Bishop of Liverpool","place":"Suffolk, England","xy":[631.4,66.8],"match":"J. C. Ryle","bio":"Ryle spent many years as a country pastor in Suffolk before becoming the first Bishop of Liverpool. He wrote in short, plain sentences so that anyone could follow. His Expository Thoughts on the Gospels go through each Gospel a few verses at a time."},{"y":"1893","name":"B. B. Warfield","life":"1851 – 1921","role":"Seminary professor","place":"Princeton, New Jersey","xy":[195.2,150.7],"match":"B. B. Warfield","bio":"Warfield taught future pastors at Princeton Seminary for over thirty years and defended the Bible as God's word. He also cared for his wife, who was an invalid, for most of their marriage, rarely leaving her side. His sermon on imitating the incarnation shows that he lived what he taught."},{"y":"1896","name":"Andrew Murray","life":"1828 – 1917","role":"Pastor and writer","place":"Wellington, South Africa","xy":[737.4,687.6],"match":"Andrew Murray","bio":"Murray was a South African pastor of Scottish family who wrote more than two hundred books on prayer and walking with God, including Abide in Christ. He led revival in his church and helped found a school for training women teachers. His books are still read by people who want to pray."},{"y":"1925","name":"G. K. Chesterton","life":"1874 – 1936","role":"Writer","place":"Beaconsfield, England","xy":[621.5,70.7],"match":"G. K. Chesterton","bio":"Chesterton was a big, cheerful journalist and storyteller, famous for his Father Brown detective stories. His books Orthodoxy and The Everlasting Man defended the faith with wit and joy. The Everlasting Man helped lead C. S. Lewis to Christ."}];
